course: dictionary
lesson: 3960
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. Furrow can be a long narrow groove cut into the soil by a plough, or a deep line on your skin.
explain: «The farmer walked along the furrows, dropping seeds into the soil.» The words farmer, seeds and soil are clues that furrow means a groove in the ground.
explain: «Her forehead furrowed as she tried to solve the puzzle.» The puzzle and the thinking are clues that furrow here means lines on the forehead from frowning. Check your answer by swapping in a meaning and seeing if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: A furrow is a long narrow groove cut into the soil by a plough.
fact: A furrow can also be a deep line or wrinkle on the skin, especially on the forehead.
fact: To furrow your brow means to make lines on your forehead by frowning, as in «He furrowed his brow.»
fact: «All ridges and furrows» means bumps and dips in the ground.
fact: The saying «to plough a lonely furrow» means to work alone without help.
exercise: «The farmer ploughed straight furrows in the field.» What is a furrow here? | A deep line on someone's forehead | A type of seed | A tall wall of stone | *A long narrow groove cut into the soil
exercise: «Grandma furrowed her brow when she read the bill.» What does furrowed mean here? | Laughed loudly | Ploughed a field | Smiled widely | *Made lines on her forehead by frowning
exercise: In «all ridges and furrows», which word means the dips? | *furrows | all | ridges | and
test: «He furrowed his brow and stared at the sum.» What does the furrowed brow show? | He was very cold | He had just finished eating | He was laughing | *He was worried or thinking hard
test: Which saying means to work alone without help? | Count the sheep | Dig a deep hole | *Plough a lonely furrow | Sow a seed in spring
exam: «Ben's forehead furrowed when he saw the bill.» What does the context tell you furrowed means? | Laughed out loud | Worried or puzzled, with lines on his forehead | Ploughed a long line in the soil | *Hung down and drooped
